---
import CopyCode from "@/components/CopyCode.astro";
import EmailLink from "@/components/EmailLink.astro";
import BaseLayout from "@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { getManifest } from "@/lib/catalog";
import { exportDate } from "@/lib/dataset";
import {
  CONTACT,
  CONTENT_LICENCE,
  CREATOR,
  GRAPHVIZ_URL,
  SITE_NAME,
  TORCHLENS,
  TORCHLENS_BIBTEX,
  TORCHLENS_CITATION,
  releaseVersion,
  siteBibtex,
  siteCitation,
} from "@/lib/site";

const manifest = getManifest();
const version = releaseVersion(manifest.export_version);
const asOf = exportDate();
const year = asOf ? Number(asOf.slice(0, 4)) : new Date().getUTCFullYear();
const tracedWith = /^\d/.test(manifest.torchlens_version) ? manifest.torchlens_version : null;

// Matches the TorchLens README quickstart at the version Menagerie pins.
const traceExample = `pip install torchlens   # install Graphviz first

import torch, torchvision.models as models, torchlens as tl
model = models.alexnet(weights=None)
log = tl.trace(model, torch.randn(1, 3, 224, 224))
log.draw()   # draws the computational graph`;

const jsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "AboutPage",
  name: `About the ${SITE_NAME}`,
  url: "https://modelmenagerie.ai/about/",
  author: { "@type": "Person", name: CREATOR.name, url: CREATOR.url },
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="What the Model Menagerie is, how each diagram is traced, checked and drawn with TorchLens, how to read one, and how to cite it."
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <article class="shell about py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-semibold sm:text-4xl">About the Model Menagerie</h1>
    <p class="lead mt-4 text-secondary">
      The Model Menagerie is an atlas of neural network architectures. Each diagram is the computational graph that
      TorchLens recorded while the model ran: every operation, in order, and the tensors passed between them.
    </p>

    <nav class="about-toc panel" aria-label="On this page">
      <ul>
        <li><a class="textlink" href="#torchlens">TorchLens</a></li>
        <li><a class="textlink" href="#method">How a diagram is made</a></li>
        <li><a class="textlink" href="#checked">What checked means</a></li>
        <li><a class="textlink" href="#status-heading">This release</a></li>
        <li><a class="textlink" href="#read">Reading a diagram</a></li>
        <li><a class="textlink" href="#cite-heading">How to cite</a></li>
        <li><a class="textlink" href="#contact">Corrections</a></li>
        <li><a class="textlink" href="#credits">Credits</a></li>
      </ul>
    </nav>

    <section aria-labelledby="torchlens">
      <h2 id="torchlens">TorchLens</h2>
      <p>
        <a class="textlink" href={TORCHLENS.repo}>TorchLens</a> is an open-source Python package for seeing, saving and
        steering PyTorch models. It captures every activation, draws the full computational graph and records detailed
        metadata for every operation, for any architecture, including dynamic and recurrent ones. It is described in a
        <a class="textlink" href={TORCHLENS.paper}>paper in Scientific Reports</a>. You can draw a model of your own the
        same way the diagrams here are drawn:
      </p>
      <div class="mt-4">
        <CopyCode code={traceExample} label="Copy the TorchLens example" name="Example: trace and draw a model" />
      </div>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="method">
      <h2 id="method">How a diagram is made</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>Build.</strong> The model is built from its published code and given an example input: a real one where
          there is one, otherwise generated data of the right shape.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Trace.</strong> TorchLens runs one forward pass and records every operation it executes, with the shape of
          every tensor.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Check.</strong> TorchLens replays the capture and compares it with the model's own output (next section).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Draw.</strong> Graphviz lays out the recorded graph, and TorchLens draws it as the diagram on the model's
          page. The PDF and the other files on the page come from the same trace.
        </li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="checked">
      <h2 id="checked">What checked means</h2>
      <p>
        For a PyTorch model, the capture is replayed operation by operation against the original forward output, and the
        graph's metadata is checked for consistency. If the replay or a check fails, the capture counts as wrong, not as a
        pass because the model ran.
      </p>
      <p>
        TorchLens also traces TensorFlow, JAX, Paddle, MLX and tinygrad models, with
        <a class="textlink" href={TORCHLENS.backends}>newer support for those frameworks</a>. Their check replays each
        captured operation from its inputs but does not rerun the whole model against a known output, so it is a weaker
        check than the one for PyTorch.
      </p>
      <p>
        Some models are too large to run. For those, TorchLens can record the graph's structure without running it. Such a
        drawing carries TorchLens's own banner, saying that its shapes and data types are hypotheses, not measurements, and
        it has no replay check.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="status" aria-labelledby="status-heading">
      <h2 id="status-heading">This release</h2>
      <p>
        {
          version
            ? `This is release ${version} of the catalog.`
            : "This is a preview release, and the site is still being built."
        }
        {" "}Every model listed here has a rendered diagram{tracedWith && `, traced with TorchLens ${tracedWith}`}. Some still
        share a stand-in drawing with other models until their own trace is redone; the listing shows no picture for
        those. The replay check is being run across the catalog, and each model's source, licence and paper are still being added;
        a model page shows them once they are recorded. The <a class="textlink" href="/dataset/">Dataset page</a> says
        what the current release contains, and the <a class="textlink" href="/changelog/">list of changes</a> says what
        changed.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="read">
      <h2 id="read">How to read a diagram</h2>
      <p>
        Each box is one operation, such as a convolution or an addition, labelled with the shape of the tensor it
        produced. Arrows show which results feed which operations, from the input to the output. Nested frames group the
        operations that belong to one module, such as a residual block. An unrolled drawing shows every pass through a
        repeated module; a rolled drawing folds the passes into one box, which keeps recurrent models compact.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="cite" aria-labelledby="cite-heading">
      <h2 id="cite-heading">How to cite</h2>
      <p>If the diagrams or the dataset help your work, please cite the Menagerie and the TorchLens paper:</p>
      <p class="citation panel">{siteCitation(year, version)}</p>
      <CopyCode code={siteBibtex(year, version)} label="Copy the BibTeX entry for the Menagerie" name="BibTeX for the Menagerie" />
      <p class="citation panel">{TORCHLENS_CITATION}</p>
      <CopyCode code={TORCHLENS_BIBTEX} label="Copy the BibTeX entry for TorchLens" name="BibTeX for TorchLens" />
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="contact">
      <h2 id="contact">Corrections and requests</h2>
      <p>
        Found a wrong or missing diagram, or a wrong fact? Open an issue on the
        <a class="textlink" href={CONTACT.issues}>TorchLens issues page</a> with the model's address. For anything you
        would rather not post in public, including a request to remove a model, email
        <EmailLink email={CONTACT.email} />.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="credits">
      <h2 id="credits">Credits</h2>
      <p>
        Every diagram is traced and drawn by <a class="textlink" href={TORCHLENS.repo}>TorchLens</a>
        (<a class="textlink" href={TORCHLENS.licence.url}>{TORCHLENS.licence.name}</a>), by JohnMark Taylor and Nikolaus
        Kriegeskorte, and laid out by <a class="textlink" href={GRAPHVIZ_URL}>Graphviz</a>. Each model's code comes from its
        authors' project, linked on its page where recorded, and keeps its own licence. The Menagerie is made by
        <a class="textlink" href={CREATOR.url}>{CREATOR.name}</a>; its text, diagrams and dataset are free to reuse under
        <a class="textlink" href={CONTENT_LICENCE.url} rel="license">{CONTENT_LICENCE.short}</a>
        (<a class="textlink" href="/legal/">privacy and licences</a>).
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .about {
    max-width: 46rem;
  }

  .about .lead {
    font-size: 1.125rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  .about-toc {
    margin-top: 2rem;
    padding: 0.25rem 1rem;
  }

  .about-toc ul {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0 1.25rem;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
  }

  /* A full tap height (44 px) for each link in the wrapping row. */
  .about-toc li,
  .about-toc a {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    min-height: 2.75rem;
  }

  .about section {
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
    scroll-margin-top: 1rem;
  }

  .about h2 {
    font-size: 1.375rem;
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .about p,
  .about ol {
    margin-top: 0.75rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  .about ol {
    padding-left: 1.4rem;
    list-style: decimal;
  }

  .about ol li + li {
    margin-top: 0.4rem;
  }

  .about .citation {
    margin-block: 1rem 0.75rem;
    padding: 1rem;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
</style>
